# Line endings, repo-wide.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows stores LF in the repository and checks out CRLF in
# the working tree. The same commit therefore produces different bytes on different
# machines, and a file's SHA-256 changes with the checkout platform.
#
# That matters here more than in most repositories, because this one treats hashes as
# evidence. Recipe reports cite the SHA-256 of every source file and step script so a
# reviewer can confirm they are reading the artifact the report describes. A hash that
# only reproduces on the machine that wrote it is not proof of anything.
#
# It also caused the generated-instruction drift that made `npm run verify` fail: the
# files under instructions/ were checked out as CRLF, the build emitted mixed endings,
# and manifest-check compared the result against the committed LF version and reported
# six files "out of sync" whose content was byte-identical.
#
# This file previously carried per-recipe blocks, added one recipe at a time as each
# hit the problem. Two recipes were listed and a third was about to be. Pinning the
# whole repository is the rule those blocks were each approximating.

# Default: treat every file as text, normalise to LF in the repository, and check out
# LF everywhere. Git still auto-detects binary content and leaves it untouched, but the
# explicit declarations below mean we do not rely on that detection.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Text, stated explicitly so intent does not depend on content sniffing -----------
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.jsonl   text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.ts      text eol=lf
*.tsx     text eol=lf
*.jsx     text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.svg     text eol=lf
*.csv     text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf
*.go      text eol=lf
*.mod     text eol=lf
*.sum     text eol=lf
*.ipynb   text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.lock    text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitkeep        text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. A CRLF shebang line makes the interpreter unreachable and
# the script fails with a confusing "bad interpreter" or "not found".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one case that genuinely requires CRLF: cmd.exe can
# mis-parse an LF-only .bat. None exist today; the rule is here so adding one is safe.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never transformed, never diffed as text ---------------------------------
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.bin  binary
*.pyc  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
*.psd  binary
*.epub binary

# --- Quarantined Tier 3: vendored CSV data is left exactly as imported ----------------
# data/mycroft-main is a vendored upstream import kept for provenance. Its CSVs were
# imported with CRLF and all 15 are stored that way; rewriting them would edit a
# provenance record to satisfy a convention they were never part of, and would show up
# as an 11,000-line diff that hides whatever else is in the change.
#
# The exemption is scoped to CSVs, NOT to the whole tree. Exempting the tree was tried
# and did the opposite of its intent: the .py/.json/.svg files under it are already
# stored LF-normalised, so `-text` made git adopt the working tree's CRLF and staged
# ~30 files as changed. These 15 CSVs are the only files there stored with CRLF, so
# `-text` is a no-op for them -- which is exactly what "leave it alone" should mean.
data/mycroft-main/**/*.csv -text

# --- Fixture files deliberately kept unparseable --------------------------------------
# These are truncated on purpose, to exercise the parse-error path of a validator. They
# are text and must stay byte-stable like everything else; the extension only keeps them
# out of the *.json globs that gate 3 and conformance use.
*.json.broken text eol=lf
